[2026-11-03T20:29:16.629Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd && git branch --show-current && git status --short && cat docs/agents/README.md && cat docs/agents/conventions.md'","aggregated_output":"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1793737712770\nissue-58-547eaf00c7c9\n# Agent workflow guide\n\nThis guide explains how an AI coding agent (or a person) picks, plans,\nimplements, verifies and hands off work in this monorepo. Start at\n[`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md) for the repository map and the required\ncommands, and follow [`conventions.md`](conventions.md) for every change. To\nwrite a new issue, read [`writing-issues.md`](writing-issues.md).\n\nIssues use one body template (Context, Goal, Scope, Dependencies, Step-by-step\nplan, Files to create or change, Acceptance criteria, How to verify, Prior art\nand libraries, References). The sections below follow that template.\n\n## Pick an issue\n\n1. Open the issue and read it completely, including every comment.\n2. Read the **Dependencies** section first. Check that every `Blocked by`\n   issue is closed (`gh issue view <N> --json state`).\n3. If a blocker is still open, work against a stub only when the issue says\n   so. Stub the blocked API with the documented signature and mark it with a\n   `TODO(#<blocker>)` comment. Never fake the blocked behaviour in tests: a\n   test that depends on it is skipped with a visible reason, not made green.\n4. If the blocker is open and the issue does not allow a stub, pick another\n   issue.\n\n## Plan\n\n- Follow the numbered **Step-by-step plan** of the issue in order.\n- One commit per step is fine; each commit should leave the tree green.\n- Keep the list of **Files to create or change** as your checklist. If you\n  need a file that is not on the list, check that it is within the issue's\n  **In scope** list first.\n- Map every **Acceptance criterion** to the step that delivers it and to the\n  check that proves it.\n\n## Write tests first\n\n- Write the test before the change and run it on its own:\n\n  ```bash\n  node --test packages/<name>/tests/<file>.test.js\n  ```\n\n  It must fail before the change and pass after it.\n\n- Repository-level tests live in `tests/*.test.js`; package tests live in\n  `packages/<name>/tests/`. `npm test` runs both.\n- Keep tests deterministic: no network, no real clock, fixed random seeds.\n- Use the shared fixtures in `docs/spec/fixtures/` when a behaviour is\n  defined by the dialect specification.\n\n## LiNo first\n\n- Every new format is Links Notation, parsed with the official\n  `links-notation` package. Never parse LiNo with regular expressions.\n- Write Links Notation in the nested form by default (#133): the links of a\n  link's values go on their own lines, indented under it, with as few\n  parentheses as possible. Code writes it with `formatLinks` of ui-core;\n  `node scripts/lino-nest.mjs <file>` converts a file, and\n  `tests/lino-nested-form.test.js` keeps the repository in this form. The\n  one-line and parenthesized forms still read the same and stay for one-line\n  places only (a table cell, a URL, a form field). See\n  [conventions](conventions.md#links-notation-in-the-nested-form).\n- For any question about the UI dialect (elements, values, ordering, IDs,\n  projection), read [`docs/spec/`](../spec/README.md) and its fixtures. If\n  the specification does not answer the question, record it in\n  [`docs/spec/open-questions.md`](../spec/open-questions.md) or open an issue;\n  do not invent new syntax silently.\n- JSON, CSS, JSX and other formats are import or export formats only.\n\n## Verify UI\n\n- Any change that renders UI needs a visual and accessibility check:\n\n  ```bash\n  npm run test:ui          # local browsers\n  npm run test:ui:docker   # the pinned container used by CI\n  ```\n\n- Update baselines only on purpose (`npm run test:ui:update`) and review\n  every changed image.\n- Attach screenshots of the changed UI to the pull request. Commit them to\n  the branch and link them with\n  `https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/blob/<branch>/<path>?raw=true`.\n- [`docs/testing-ui.md`](../testing-ui.md) explains projects, baselines and\n  the axe-core audit.\n\n## Licensing\n\n- Read [`docs/licensing.md`](../licensing.md) before adding a dependency or an\n  asset. Only permissive licences are allowed (MIT, ISC, BSD, Apache-2.0,\n  CC0, CC-BY with attribution recorded).\n- Record every third-party asset in `assets/assets.lino` and run:\n\n  ```bash\n  npm run check:assets\n  ```\n\n## Changesets\n\n- A change to a published package under `packages/` needs a changeset:\n\n  ```bash\n  npx changeset\n  ```\n\n  Pick the package (for example `@link-foundation/ui-core`) and the bump\n  type. Commit the generated file in `.changeset/`.\n\n- Documentation-only changes do not need one. See\n  [`docs/monorepo.md`](../monorepo.md) and\n  [`docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`](../CONTRIBUTING.md) for the rules the CI guard\n  enforces.\n\n## Hand-off\n\n- Run the four required commands from [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md) on the\n  exact revision you push.\n- The pull request description starts with `Fixes #<N>` (one line per issue\n  it closes) and lists every acceptance criterion with its evidence: the\n  command and its output, a test name, or a screenshot.\n- Fill in the [pull request template](../../.github/pull_request_template.md)\n  checklist. Leave an unmet criterion unchecked and say why; never present\n  blocked work as done.\n- Wait for CI to be green before marking the pull request as ready.\n\n## When stuck\n\n- Do not widen the scope of the pull request. Open a\n  [new issue](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues/new) of type Bug or\n  Feature, written as [writing-issues.md](writing-issues.md) describes, and\n  link it from the pull request.\n- If a blocker turns out to be missing, say so in the pull request and in the\n  new issue, with the evidence (logs, failing command, CI run URL).\n- If the issue itself is unclear, write down the assumption you chose in the\n  pull request description.\n# Conventions for every change\n\nThese rules apply to every change in this repository, made by a person or by an\nAI agent. They are the canonical copy of the conventions footer that the\nroadmap issues carry\n([`docs/case-studies/issue-1/roadmap/issue-footer.md`](../case-studies/issue-1/roadmap/issue-footer.md),\nkept unchanged as a case-study record). When the two differ, this file wins.\n\nThe roadmap was planned in\n[issue #1](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues/1). The full plan, the\nrequirement list and the research are in the\n[issue 1 case study](../case-studies/issue-1/README.md), and the target design\nis in [`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`](../ARCHITECTURE.md).\n\n- **Read first:** [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md),\n  [`docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`](../CONTRIBUTING.md),\n  [`docs/javascript-first.md`](../javascript-first.md) and\n  [`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`](../ARCHITECTURE.md).\n- **Links Notation first.** Every new format (documents, configuration,\n  tokens, styles, animations, recordings, asset records) is Links Notation\n  parsed with the official `links-notation` package (or the official port for\n  Swift and Kotlin). Never hand-write a LiNo parser with regular expressions.\n  JSON, CSS, JSX and so on are only import or export formats. Write it in the\n  [nested form](#links-notation-in-the-nested-form).\n- **JavaScript first.** Plain ES modules (`.js`/`.mjs`) with JSDoc types;\n  TypeScript declaration files (`.d.ts`) are allowed for public APIs. Swift and\n  Kotlin ports must pass the same shared fixtures as the JavaScript code.\n- **Tests:** `node --test` (see `package.json`). Every behaviour change has a\n  test that fails before the change. Keep tests deterministic: no network, no\n  real clock (inject time), no random seeds without a fixed value.\n- **Quality gates:** `npm run lint`, `npm run format:check`,\n  `npm run check:duplication` and `npm test` must pass. Files stay under\n  1,500 lines (`scripts/check-file-line-limits.sh`).\n- **Releases:** a change to a published package needs a changeset\n  (`npx changeset`), as described in\n  [`docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`](../CONTRIBUTING.md) and\n  [`docs/monorepo.md`](../monorepo.md).\n- **Licensing:** the repository is public domain (Unlicense). Our own assets\n  are CC0-1.0. A third-party dependency or asset must use a permissive licence\n  (MIT, ISC, BSD, Apache-2.0, CC0, CC-BY with attribution recorded) and be\n  recorded as described in [`docs/licensing.md`](../licensing.md). Do not add\n  GPL, AGPL, non-commercial or \"source available\" code or assets.\n- **Accessibility:** interactive UI follows the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices,\n  works with the keyboard, respects `prefers-reduced-motion` and\n  `prefers-color-scheme`, and meets WCAG 2.2 AA contrast.\n- **Scope discipline:** do only what the issue asks. If you find a defect or\n  a missing prerequisite, open a new issue (type Bug or Feature) and link it,\n  instead of widening the pull request.\n- **Done means:** every acceptance criterion is checked, CI is green, and the\n  pull request description says `Fixes #<issue>` and shows how each\n  criterion was verified (command output or screenshots for UI).\n\n## Links Notation in the nested form\n\nLinks Notation is written in the **nested form** by default, in documents,\nexamples and code ([#133](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues/133)).\nA link goes on one line with its atom values. Each value that is itself a\nlink with values goes on its own line, indented two spaces under it.\nParentheses are left out wherever indentation makes them unneeded:\n\n```lino\nok: button\n  label OK\n  on press\n    navigate home\n```\n\nand not the multi-line parenthesized form\n\n```text\n(ok: button\n  (label OK)\n  (on press (navigate home)))\n```\n\n- All forms read the same, and ui-core keeps reading the one-line and\n  parenthesized forms. Only the form that is written changes.\n- Code writes Links Notation with `formatLinks` of ui-core, whose default\n  style is the nested form. It takes raw values and `links-notation` links.\n  Do not call `Link.format()` or join parentheses by hand.\n- A link of links with no head, such as one tooltip field, needs\n  parentheses: `(field product) (type nominal)`. Parentheses also stay\n  wherever the text must fit on one line: a Markdown table cell, a URL, a\n  one-line form field or a diagnostic message.\n- `node scripts/lino-nest.mjs <file.lino|file.md>...` rewrites a file in the\n  nested form (`--check` only reports). `tests/lino-nested-form.test.js`\n  checks every `.lino` file and every ` ```lino ` block. Its allowlist\n  covers the files that keep the other forms on purpose: the spec fixtures,\n  the reader fixtures and the history.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
