[2026-11-03T20:29:23.219Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat docs/CONTRIBUTING.md docs/javascript-first.md docs/ARCHITECTURE.md && cat package.json && git log -5 --oneline && rg --files -g AGENTS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Contributing to link-foundation/ui\n\n## Development Workflow\n\nUse Node.js 24 with npm 10.9 or newer for development and release tooling.\nChangesets 3 requires a supported Node.js 22.11, 24, or 26+ runtime.\n\n1. **Fork the repository** and clone your fork\n2. **Create a feature branch**: `git checkout -b feature/my-feature`\n3. **Install dependencies**: `bun install` (or `npm install`)\n4. **Make your changes**\n5. **Run local checks**: `bun run check`\n6. **Create a changeset**: `bun run changeset`\n7. **Commit and push** (pre-commit hooks will run automatically)\n8. **Create a Pull Request**\n\n### For AI agents\n\nMost issues in this repository are solved by AI coding agents. Agents (and\npeople working the same way) start here:\n\n- [`AGENTS.md`](../AGENTS.md): the entry point, with the repository map and\n  the commands that must pass. [`CLAUDE.md`](../CLAUDE.md) points to it.\n- [`docs/agents/README.md`](agents/README.md): how to pick, plan, implement,\n  verify and hand off an issue.\n- [`docs/agents/conventions.md`](agents/conventions.md): the conventions every\n  change follows (Links Notation first, tests, quality gates, licensing,\n  accessibility, scope, \"done means\").\n- [`docs/agents/writing-issues.md`](agents/writing-issues.md): how to write an\n  issue that a less capable agent can solve.\n- New issues are plain\n  [GitHub issues](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues/new) (type Bug\n  or Feature) written as `docs/agents/writing-issues.md` describes; this\n  repository has no issue templates (`.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/` was removed in\n  [#133](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues/133)). Pull requests use\n  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, and `tests/contributor-guide.test.js`\n  checks it and the agent guide.\n\n### Case study folders\n\n`docs/case-studies/issue-N/` is only for issue `N` of\n[link-foundation/ui](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues). Research\ninherited from the template repository lives in\n[`docs/template-history/`](template-history/README.md), where issue numbers\nrefer to the template repository's issues. Do not add new files there.\n\n## Code Standards\n\n### File Size Limits\n\n**Maximum 1500 lines per file** (enforced via ESLint `max-lines` rule and CI `check-file-line-limits` job).\n\nThis benefits both AI and human developers by ensuring files remain readable and maintainable.\n\n### Formatting and Linting\n\nAll code must pass:\n\n```bash\n# Check formatting\nbun run format:check\n\n# Check linting\nbun run lint\n\n# Run all checks\nbun run check\n```\n\nThe pre-commit hook runs automatically, but you can also manually format and fix:\n\n```bash\n# Auto-fix formatting\nbun run format\n\n# Auto-fix lint issues\nbun run lint:fix\n```\n\n### Testing Requirements\n\nTests should:\n\n- Cover critical paths\n- Work across all runtimes (Node.js, Bun, Deno)\n- Use the [test-anywhere](https://github.com/link-foundation/test-anywhere) framework\n\n```bash\n# Run tests\nbun test --timeout 30000\nnpm test\ndeno test -A --parallel\n```\n\n## Monorepo Layout\n\nThe repository root is a private npm workspaces monorepo. Publishable code\nlives in `packages/*`, starting with `@link-foundation/ui-core`. See\n[docs/monorepo.md](monorepo.md) for the layout, how to add a package, naming,\nversioning and changesets.\n\n## Version Management with Changesets\n\nThis project uses [Changesets](https://github.com/changesets/changesets) to manage versions and changelogs. This eliminates merge conflicts that occur when multiple PRs bump the version in `package.json`.\n\n### Adding a Changeset\n\nWhen you make changes that affect users, add a changeset:\n\n```bash\nbun run changeset\n# or\nnpm run changeset\n```\n\nThis will prompt you to:\n\n1. Select the type of change (patch/minor/major)\n2. Provide a summary of the changes\n\nThe changeset will be saved as a markdown file in `.changeset/` and should be committed with your PR.\n\n### Changeset Guidelines\n\n| Type      | When to Use                          | Examples                                            |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Patch** | Bug fixes, internal changes          | Fix typo, update dependency, refactor internal code |\n| **Minor** | New features, non-breaking additions | Add new function, new optional parameter            |\n| **Major** | Breaking changes                     | Remove function, change API signature               |\n\nExample changeset summary:\n\n```markdown\nAdd support for custom configuration via config file\n```\n\n### What Changes Need Changesets?\n\n**Required** for:\n\n- Bug fixes\n- New features\n- Breaking changes\n- Public API changes\n\n**Not required** for:\n\n- Documentation-only changes (in `./docs` folder)\n- Changes to markdown files\n- CI/CD workflow updates (unless they affect users)\n\n### PR guard comparison and local validation\n\nVersion and changeset guards compare the PR head with its merge base. CI passes\n`GITHUB_BASE_SHA` and `GITHUB_HEAD_SHA`; local runs default to `origin/main` and\n`HEAD`. Fetch the base before running the guards. Missing refs, Git failures,\nand invalid package metadata fail validation.\n\nThe changeset guard counts added fragments only. Exact file moves do not count\nas new fragments; replacing a deleted fragment with different content does.\nBoth paths of a move are checked when deciding whether code requires a fragment.\nMarkdown, `docs/`, `examples/`,\n`experiments/`, `dev/log/`, and changeset metadata are exempt. In multi-language\nrepositories, unrelated language roots are exempt; shared workflows still\nrequire a fragment. For local directory validation without Git comparison, use\n`ALLOW_LOCAL_CHANGESET_SCAN=true node scripts/validate-changeset.mjs`. This\nexplicit mode is forbidden in CI. Set `DEBUG=1` to print comparison details.\n\nAutomated release PRs are exempt only when their author matches the release\nactor, both repositories match `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`, and their branch uses a\nrelease prefix. The default author is `github-actions[bot]`. When a dedicated\nrelease token creates PRs as another account, configure repository variable\n`RELEASE_PR_ACTOR` to that account's exact login. A branch name alone never\ngrants an exemption.\n\n## Release Process\n\nThe release process is fully automated:\n\n1. **PR with changeset merged** - The changeset is added to `.changeset/`\n2. **CI detects changesets** - On push to main, CI checks for pending changesets\n3. **Version bump** - Package version is updated based on changeset type\n4. **Changelog update** - `CHANGELOG.md` is updated automatically\n5. **npm publish** - Package is published via OIDC trusted publishing\n6. **GitHub Release** - A release is created with formatted notes\n\nChangesets explicitly uses Prettier in `.changeset/config.json`, matching\n`format:check`. Keep that setting when adding runtime configs: auto-detection\nselects Deno for any `deno.json`, independently of the npm package-manager\ndeclaration. PR lint checks dry-run `npm run changeset:version` after the fresh\nmerge simulation and restore the generated metadata and consumed fragments.\nThis requires no publishing credentials and runs independently of release\ncredential preflight.\n\n### Multiple Changesets\n\nIf multiple PRs are merged before a release:\n\n- All changesets are merged into one\n- The highest version bump type wins (major > minor > patch)\n- All descriptions are preserved in chronological order\n\n## Pull Request Guidelines\n\n### PR Title\n\nUse a clear, descriptive title that summarizes the change:\n\n```\nfeat: Add support for custom configuration\nfix: Resolve race condition in async handler\ndocs: Update API documentation for v2\n```\n\n### PR Description\n\nInclude:\n\n- **What** - Describe the change\n- **Why** - Explain the motivation\n- **Testing** - How the change was tested\n\n### CI Checks\n\nAll PRs must pass:\n\n- [ ] Test compilation (syntax check)\n- [ ] Lint, format, and secrets scan\n- [ ] File line limits check\n- [ ] Changeset validation (for code changes)\n- [ ] Documentation validation (when docs change)\n- [ ] Tests on all platforms (Ubuntu, macOS, Windows)\n- [ ] Tests on all runtimes (Node.js, Bun, Deno)\n\n### Fresh Merge Simulation\n\nThe CI workflow automatically:\n\n1. Merges the latest `main` into your PR branch\n2. Runs checks against the merged state\n\nIf this fails with merge conflicts, you need to:\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin main\ngit merge origin/main\n# Resolve conflicts\ngit push\n```\n\n## Questions?\n\nIf you have questions about contributing, feel free to open an issue for discussion.\n\n## JavaScript-first mixed-language projects\n\nFollow [the JavaScript-first gate and single push workflow](javascript-first.md) before enabling production Rust jobs for a mixed-language project.\n\nStandalone shell scripts and Husky hooks are checked with ShellCheck 0.11.0.\nInstall [pipx](https://pipx.pypa.io/stable/how-to/install-pipx.html) for the staged-file hook;\nit runs the same pinned `shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1` package as CI. To check all shell\nfiles locally, run:\n\n```sh\ngit ls-files -z '*.sh' '.husky/*' | xargs -0 pipx run --spec shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1 shellcheck\n```\n\nRelease scripts load `command-stream` and `lino-arguments` from installed,\nlockfile-pinned development dependencies. Run `npm ci` before invoking them;\nthey do not download or install helpers at runtime. Keep both `package-lock.json`\nand `deno.lock` synchronized when updating these dependencies. CI runs its pinned\nsecrets scan after Node setup and before installing project dependencies, so the\nscanner and its preset resolve from the same npm execution tree.\n\nShell scripts and Git hooks use LF on every platform through `.gitattributes`.\nThe prepare script calls Husky's installed API and verifies `core.hooksPath`.\nSet `CI_SCRIPTS_DEBUG=1` for optional dependency and hook installation tracing.\n# JavaScript first and the single push gate\n\nFor mixed Rust/JavaScript projects, implement and debug complete behavior in native\nJavaScript before compiling Rust. A browser UI, WASM wrapper, source envelope,\nmatching filename set or equal test count does not establish behavioral parity.\n\nMaintain a feature inventory covering public APIs, CLI commands, errors, streams,\npersistence and deployment contracts. Record missing, partial and carried items;\nthe production Rust gate must reject each. Use shared input/output fixtures and\nindependent native execution, including refusal and boundary cases.\n\nPin translation source and dependencies by immutable revision. Preserve typed meta IR (including Links\nNotation) intermediates, source hashes, target artifacts and refusal reasons.\nRegenerate into temporary output and compare tracked targets before Rust builds.\nA successful source round trip establishes preservation of source text; execution\nparity still requires observations from both native runtimes. Handwritten native\nadapters must be explicit inventory entries with their own tests.\n\nUse `.github/workflows/javascript-first-gate.yml` as a callable workflow. Supply\nreal repository commands for the native JavaScript suite, strict readiness/parity\ninventory check and regenerate-and-diff check. The template deliberately supplies\nno default generator or placeholder successful command. Depend on its successful\njob with `needs:` before every Rust test/build/package/container/publish job;\nconsume its `tested-sha` output as the checkout ref. A skipped gate is not success.\nFor projects whose JavaScript port is unfinished, keep the readiness gate red and\nreport the blockers rather than representing the rollout as complete. Adoption of\nthis opt-in workflow does not by itself migrate existing Rust applications.\n\nRun untrusted candidates through `pull_request` so runner caches stay in the PR\nscope. A manual or release caller must establish that its selected candidate is\ntrusted before executing it in a default-branch context. Never pass an unvalidated\ndispatch input as `source-sha`: read-only repository permissions and disabled\ndependency caching still leave the runner's cache credentials accessible to code.\n\nThe [working mixed-language example](../examples/javascript-first/README.md)\nsupplies actual native tests, strict inventory and pinned translation commands.\nIts caller exercises every downstream Rust path with the gate's tested SHA.\n\nAvoid `workflow_run` when same-workflow `needs:` or reusable workflows suffice.\nIf a separate workflow is required, check the source conclusion, repository/event\ntrust, exact `head_sha` and artifact provenance; default `GITHUB_SHA` refers to the\ndefault branch rather than the initiating run. Keep untrusted PR execution read-only.\n\nRead-only CI checks may run in parallel after the JavaScript prerequisite. Give\nactive releases, deploys, tag writes and generated-content pushes one shared\nrepository writer group with `cancel-in-progress: false`; check cancellation must\nnot interrupt publication. Reconcile current remote state before each write.\n\nDraft changes in bulk. Workers may inspect, edit and run bounded JavaScript checks,\nbut exactly one coordinator owns git push. That coordinator stages a complete\nbatch, verifies the exact candidate revision with required local checks, inspects\nthe diff and pushes once. Wait for required same-revision CI before another push;\ncombine all observed failures into the next batch. Push permission does not imply\npermission to merge, publish, deploy or clean user-owned caches.\n\nBound expensive local work: avoid duplicate profile/target/feature configurations,\nshare compatible build caches, limit compiler processes, measure wall time and disk,\nand stop when the agreed budget is exhausted. Never silently reduce production\nassertions, treat partial runs as green, or delete unrelated caches to regain space.\n\nReferences: [router issue 759](https://github.com/link-assistant/router/issues/759),\n[GitHub needs semantics](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax#jobsjob_idneeds),\n[GitHub workflow_run](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#workflow_run),\n[Cargo build cache](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/build-cache.html).\n# Architecture (proposal)\n\nThis document is the target architecture of the associative UI framework\nplanned in [issue #1](https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues/1). It is a\n**proposal**: every part of it is delivered by a roadmap issue listed in\n[`ROADMAP.md`](ROADMAP.md), and each roadmap issue may refine it. The evidence\nbehind each decision is in the\n[issue 1 case study](case-studies/issue-1/README.md).\n\n## Principles\n\n1. **Links first.** Every UI concept (element, property, style rule, design\n   token, theme, behaviour, data binding, animation, recording, configuration\n   override, asset licence) is a link in a links network. Links Notation\n   (LiNo, [link-foundation/links-notation](https://github.com/link-foundation/links-notation))\n   is its only canonical text form. JSON, CSS, JSX, XAML, SwiftUI and so on are\n   import or export formats, never the source of truth.\n2. **One dialect.** Structure, configuration, styles, tokens, animations and\n   recordings use one LiNo vocabulary (the _UI dialect_), so any other UI\n   framework can be described as a LiNo document in that dialect.\n3. **Sharable by default.** A UI, a theme, a set of overrides or a recording is\n   a plain LiNo text that can be pasted into an issue, a chat with an agent,\n   or a URL.\n4. **Runtime interpreter, thin renderers.** One platform-neutral core\n   interprets the links network. Each platform (DOM, React, Vue, React Native,\n   SwiftUI, Jetpack Compose) has a thin renderer. Code generation is used only\n   for wrappers, types and optional \"eject to code\" exports.\n5. **JavaScript first.** As required by [`javascript-first.md`](javascript-first.md),\n   the JavaScript implementation and shared conformance fixtures come first.\n   Swift and Kotlin ports must pass the same fixtures.\n6. **Everything editable at runtime.** Every rendered element keeps a stable\n   link ID that maps back to its LiNo source. This is what makes the\n   development/configuration mode and the WYSIWYG designer possible on any\n   site built with the framework.\n7. **Public domain.** Code is released under the [Unlicense](../LICENSE). Our\n   own non-code assets are dedicated to the public domain (CC0-1.0).\n   Third-party assets are allowed only with a machine-readable record of the\n   source, copyright holder, licence (SPDX ID) and attribution text.\n\n## Layers\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n  subgraph Sources[\"LiNo sources (sharable text)\"]\n    Doc[\"documents<br/>(structure)\"]\n    Tok[\"tokens & themes\"]\n    Sty[\"styles\"]\n    Ani[\"animations & recordings\"]\n    Cfg[\"configuration overrides\"]\n    Ast[\"asset records\"]\n  end\n  Sources --> Core[\"ui-core<br/>parse · validate · project · diff/patch\"]\n  Core --> Store[\"links store<br/>(in-memory network, transactions, undo/redo,<br/>IndexedDB / doublets-web persistence)\"]\n  Store --> Config[\"configuration layers<br/>defaults < theme < site < user < session\"]\n  Config --> Engine[\"style & token resolver,<br/>behaviour state machines, animation engine\"]\n  Engine --> DOM[\"DOM renderer<br/>(custom elements)\"]\n  DOM --> React[\"React adapter\"]\n  DOM --> Vue[\"Vue adapter\"]\n  Engine --> RN[\"React Native renderer\"]\n  Engine --> Swift[\"SwiftUI renderer\"]\n  Engine --> Kotlin[\"Compose renderer\"]\n  Store --> Dev[\"dev/configuration mode<br/>(inspector overlay)\"]\n  Dev --> Designer[\"WYSIWYG designer\"]\n  Dev --> Export[\"export overrides as LiNo<br/>(agent hand-off, share URL)\"]\n```\n\n| Layer                 | Package (working name)                         | Responsibility                                                                                                                                  |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Dialect specification | `docs/spec/`                                   | Vocabulary, grammar of the UI dialect on top of LiNo, conformance fixtures shared by every implementation.                                      |\n| Core                  | `@link-foundation/ui-core`                     | Parse LiNo (via `links-notation`), project links into an element tree, validate against the dialect, format back, diff and patch.               |\n| Store                 | `@link-foundation/ui-store`                    | Mutable links network with stable IDs, change events, transactions, undo/redo, persistence (IndexedDB; `doublets-web` compatible).              |\n| Links                 | `@link-foundation/ui-links`                    | Associative doublets storages behind one interface, link-cli substitution queries, types and permissions as links, links server, synced client. |\n| Configuration         | `@link-foundation/ui-config`                   | Layered configuration and overrides; export of overrides as LiNo patches.                                                                       |\n| Settings              | `@link-foundation/ui-settings`                 | Settings schemas in LiNo: validation, presets, migrations, diff-only values documents and share links, before-paint application.                |\n| Tokens and themes     | `@link-foundation/ui-tokens`                   | Design tokens in LiNo; export to CSS custom properties, DTCG 2025.10 JSON, Swift and Kotlin.                                                    |\n| Styles                | `@link-foundation/ui-style`                    | Style rules as links (selectors, states, responsive conditions) resolved against tokens.                                                        |\n| DOM renderer          | `@link-foundation/ui-dom`                      | Reference renderer with custom elements and incremental updates.                                                                                |\n| Framework adapters    | `ui-react`, `ui-vue`, `ui-react-native`        | Thin bindings over the core and DOM renderer (React Native renders natively).                                                                   |\n| Native ports          | `ui-swift` (SwiftPM), `ui-kotlin` (Gradle/KMP) | SwiftUI and Jetpack Compose renderers of the same dialect.                                                                                      |\n| Components            | `@link-foundation/ui-components`               | Controls, layout, chat, charts, diagrams, links-network visualisation; behaviour as state machines described in LiNo.                           |\n| Animation             | `@link-foundation/ui-animation`                | Keyframe/spring/timeline model, player (Web Animations API), recorder (timestamped LiNo events).                                                |\n| Dev mode              | `@link-foundation/ui-devmode`                  | Runtime inspector overlay, property editing, persistence, \"make these the defaults\" export.                                                     |\n| Designer              | `@link-foundation/ui-designer`                 | Visual Studio-like WYSIWYG designer built with the framework itself.                                                                            |\n| Converters            | `@link-foundation/ui-convert`                  | Importers (HTML/DOM, JSX/MUI, Vue SFC, Mermaid, Vega-Lite, Lottie, DTCG) and exporters.                                                         |\n| Assets                | `assets/` + `assets.lino`                      | Own CC0 assets and third-party asset records with SPDX licences.                                                                                |\n\nPackage names are working names; the monorepo issue decides the final ones.\n\n## The UI dialect (sketch)\n\nThe samples below are in\n[`case-studies/issue-1/samples/`](case-studies/issue-1/samples/) and are\nchecked by [`experiments/issue-1/lino-ui-dialect-roundtrip.mjs`](../experiments/issue-1/lino-ui-dialect-roundtrip.mjs):\n`links-notation` 0.27.1 parses every sample and formats it back to an equal\nlinks network.\n\nA document (structure, configuration and behaviour in one place):\n\n```lino\napp: document\n  title 'Links UI demo'\n  theme material3\n  body: stack\n    direction vertical\n    gap\n      token space.medium\n    greeting: text 'Hello, links'\n    ok: button\n      label 'OK'\n      variant filled\n      on press\n        navigate home\n```\n\nTokens and themes:\n\n```lino\nmaterial3: theme\n  extends base\n  token color.primary '#6750A4'\n  token space.medium 16px\n  token motion.duration.short 150ms\n```\n\nAnimation and a recording:\n\n```lino\npress-feedback: animation\n  target ok\n  duration\n    token motion.duration.short\n  track scale\n    key 0 1\n    key 0.5 0.96\n    key 1 1\nrecording-1: recording\n  event 0ms\n    set ok label 'OK'\n  event 840ms\n    input pointer down ok\n  event 990ms\n    set ok label 'Saved'\n```\n\nConfiguration overrides produced by the dev mode (what a user hands to an\nagent with \"make these the defaults\"):\n\n```lino\nuser-overrides: overrides\n  layer user\n  base app\n  set greeting text 'Hello, agents'\n  set material3 token color.primary '#00639B'\n```\n\n### Links Notation parse shapes\n\nThe experiment\n[`experiments/issue-1/lino-parse-shapes.mjs`](../experiments/issue-1/lino-parse-shapes.mjs)\nshows that `links-notation` 0.27.1 returns **different link structures** for\nthe same element depending on how it is written:\n\n- `(ok: button (label 'OK'))` on one line gives one nested link;\n- the same text split over several lines inside parentheses, and the indented\n  form `ok: button` / `  label 'OK'`, are returned by `Parser.parse()` as\n  expanded path links such as `((ok: button) (label OK))`;\n- `Parser.parseGroups()` keeps the indented form as an explicit tree.\n\n`ui-core` therefore defines one **tree projection** that accepts all three\nforms and produces the same element tree. The dialect specification issue\nfixes this projection and its conformance fixtures.\n\n## Configuration layers and agent hand-off\n\nEvery value an element uses is resolved through ordered layers:\n\n1. `defaults` — the component's own defaults (shipped in the package);\n2. `theme` — the active theme(s), which may be nested per subtree;\n3. `site` — the application's committed LiNo configuration;\n4. `user` — overrides saved in the browser (IndexedDB);\n5. `session` — temporary overrides made in dev mode and not yet saved.\n\nDev mode writes only to `user` or `session`. \"Make these the defaults\" exports\nthe difference between the top layers and `site` as an `overrides` LiNo\ndocument, a share URL, and a ready-to-paste agent instruction. The agent then\napplies the patch to the committed `site` configuration.\n\n## Development/configuration mode and designer\n\n- Enter dev mode with a keyboard shortcut, a `?ui-dev` URL parameter, or a\n  `<ui-dev-mode>` element. Sites can disable or restrict it.\n- Hovering highlights elements and clicking selects them. The inspector shows\n  the outline tree (links network), the property grid (resolved value plus the\n  layer it came from), styles, tokens, animations and the LiNo source.\n- The WYSIWYG designer reuses the inspector and adds a toolbox, canvas drag\n  and drop, alignment guides, multi-select, undo/redo, docked panels,\n  two-way source sync and a timeline. It is built with the framework itself.\n\n## Themes and other frameworks as LiNo\n\nA theme is a LiNo package of tokens, style rules and component templates\n(the XAML control-template idea). Material 3, Fluent 2, Apple HIG-guided and\n\"classic\" themes (deepcase versions, konard.github.io styles, retro desktop\nlooks) are just alternative packages. Migration from MUI, Fluent UI, shadcn/ui\nand other frameworks is a converter plus a mapping table into the same\ndialect.\n\nThe shared component catalogue and token taxonomy live in [`docs/guidelines/`](guidelines/README.md).\n\n## Platforms\n\n- **Web:** custom elements (plain JavaScript), React and Vue wrappers over the\n  same core.\n- **React Native:** a renderer that maps dialect elements to React Native\n  primitives (New Architecture).\n- **Swift:** a SwiftPM package with a SwiftUI renderer. It reads LiNo with a\n  Swift parser (an upstream `links-notation` port, or a port created by the\n  roadmap if none exists).\n- **Kotlin:** a Gradle/Kotlin Multiplatform package with a Jetpack Compose /\n  Compose Multiplatform renderer, with the same parser condition.\n\nAll implementations run the shared conformance fixtures from `docs/spec/`.\n\n## Associative technology stack usage\n\n| Component                                                                     | Use in this framework                                                    |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| [links-notation](https://github.com/link-foundation/links-notation)           | Canonical text format; parser and formatter for every implementation.    |\n| [lino-objects-codec](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-objects-codec)   | Strict encoding and decoding of object-shaped configuration.             |\n| [lino-arguments](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-arguments)           | CLI, environment and `.lenv` configuration of tools (converters, build). |\n| [lino-i18n](https://github.com/link-foundation/lino-i18n)                     | Localisation catalogs for every component and the designer.              |\n| [meta-language](https://github.com/link-foundation/meta-language)             | Lossless CST networks for importing source code (JSX, Vue SFC, HTML).    |\n| [link-cli](https://github.com/link-foundation/link-cli)                       | Query and substitution conventions for selectors and patches.            |\n| [doublets-web](https://github.com/linksplatform/doublets-web)                 | Compatibility target for browser persistence of the links store.         |\n| [relative-meta-logic](https://github.com/link-foundation/relative-meta-logic) | Context-relative values (per theme, per user, per device) as a model.    |\n\nThe full mapping, including which components are direct dependencies, is in\nthe case study.\n{\n  \"name\": \"@link-foundation/ui\",\n  \"version\": \"0.11.31\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"description\": \"Associative UI framework monorepo: npm workspaces under packages/ whose documents, configuration, styles and animations are written in Links Notation\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"workspaces\": [\n    \"packages/*\"\n  ],\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"node --test --test-timeout=30000 tests/*.test.js packages/*/tests/*.test.js packages/*/tests/*/*.test.js\",\n    \"test:ui\": \"playwright test\",\n    \"test:ui:update\": \"playwright test --update-snapshots\",\n    \"test:ui:docker\": \"docker run --rm -v \\\"$PWD\\\":[REDACTED] -w /work -e CI=1 mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.59.1-noble sh -c 'npm ci && npx playwright test'\",\n    \"check:playwright\": \"node scripts/check-playwright-version.mjs\",\n    \"lint\": \"node scripts/lint.mjs\",\n    \"lint:all\": \"eslint .\",\n    \"lint:fix\": \"eslint . --fix\",\n    \"format\": \"prettier --write .\",\n    \"format:check\": \"prettier --check .\",\n    \"check:duplication\": \"jscpd .\",\n    \"check:assets\": \"node scripts/check-assets.mjs\",\n    \"assets:attributions\": \"node scripts/render-attributions.mjs\",\n    \"build:icons\": \"node scripts/build-icons.mjs\",\n    \"check:icons\": \"node scripts/build-icons.mjs --check\",\n    \"check\": \"npm run lint && npm run format:check && npm run check:duplication\",\n    \"example:web:dev\": \"npm --prefix examples/universal-app run dev\",\n    \"example:web:build\": \"npm --prefix examples/universal-app run build\",\n    \"example:web:preview\": \"npm --prefix examples/universal-app run preview\",\n    \"example:web:preview-images\": \"node scripts/update-preview-images.mjs\",\n    \"example:desktop:package\": \"npm --prefix examples/universal-app run desktop:package\",\n    \"example:desktop:make\": \"npm --prefix examples/universal-app run desktop:make\",\n    \"example:mobile:sync\": \"npm --prefix examples/universal-app run mobile:sync\",\n    \"prepare\": \"node scripts/install-git-hooks.mjs\",\n    \"changeset\": \"changeset\",\n    \"changeset:version\": \"node scripts/changeset-version.mjs\",\n    \"changeset:publish\": \"changeset publish\",\n    \"changeset:status\": \"changeset status --since=origin/main\",\n    \"check:spec\": \"node scripts/check-spec-fixtures.mjs\",\n    \"check:i18n\": \"node packages/ui-i18n/scripts/check-i18n.mjs\"\n  },\n  \"keywords\": [\n    \"ui\",\n    \"links-notation\",\n    \"associative\",\n    \"design-system\",\n    \"wysiwyg\"\n  ],\n  \"author\": \"\",\n  \"license\": \"Unlicense\",\n  \"devEngines\": {\n    \"packageManager\": {\n      \"name\": \"npm\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"repository\": {\n    \"type\": \"git\",\n    \"url\": \"git+https://github.com/link-foundation/ui.git\"\n  },\n  \"homepage\": \"https://github.com/link-foundation/ui#readme\",\n  \"bugs\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://github.com/link-foundation/ui/issues\"\n  },\n  \"engines\": {\n    \"node\": \">=20.10.0\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@axe-core/playwright\": \"4.14.0\",\n    \"@changesets/cli\": \"^3.0.3\",\n    \"@eslint/js\": \"^10.0.1\",\n    \"@playwright/test\": \"1.59.1\",\n    \"axe-core\": \"4.14.0\",\n    \"command-stream\": \"2.4.0\",\n    \"esbuild\": \"0.27.0\",\n    \"eslint\": \"^10.13.0\",\n    \"eslint-config-prettier\": \"^10.1.8\",\n    \"eslint-plugin-prettier\": \"^5.5.4\",\n    \"husky\": \"^9.1.7\",\n    \"jscpd\": \"^5.4.0\",\n    \"links-notation\": \"^0.27.1\",\n    \"lino-arguments\": \"0.4.0\",\n    \"lint-staged\": \"^16.4.0\",\n    \"prettier\": \"^3.6.2\",\n    \"svgo\": \"^4.1.0\",\n    \"test-anywhere\": \"^0.8.48\"\n  },\n  \"lint-staged\": {\n    \"*.{js,mjs,cjs}\": [\n      \"eslint --fix --max-warnings 0 --no-warn-ignored\",\n      \"prettier --write\",\n      \"prettier --check\"\n    ],\n    \"*.md\": [\n      \"prettier --write\",\n      \"prettier --check\"\n    ],\n    \"*.sh\": [\n      \"pipx run --spec shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1 shellcheck\"\n    ],\n    \".husky/*\": [\n      \"pipx run --spec shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1 shellcheck\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\nde4bae9 Initial commit with task details\n77db8d2 Merge pull request #146 from link-foundation/issue-55-e6e607e6af35\n80336cb Merge pull request #143 from link-foundation/issue-53-12f6447387cf\nb58a2a0 Merge pull request #142 from link-foundation/issue-47-2a7dfd0fd619\nfcbd93c Revert \"Initial commit with task details\"\nAGENTS.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
